(function (T) { T.unit({ id: "7.3", title: "Block programming: build a game in Scratch", q: "How do sequence, selection and iteration combine to make a working program?", lessons: [
{ t: "A tour of Scratch", o: "I can find my way around Scratch and position a sprite using coordinates.",
  k: ["sprite: a character or object that can be programmed", "stage: the area where sprites move and the program is seen", "coordinates: two numbers (x, y) that give a position"],
  d: ["On a graph, where is the point (0, 0)?", "At the centre, where the x and y axes cross (the origin)."],
  s: ["The Scratch screen|Stage: where the action happens|Sprite list: the characters|Block palette: instructions grouped by colour|Script area: where blocks snap together",
      "The stage is a grid|x goes from -240 (left) to 240 (right)|y goes from -180 (bottom) to 180 (top)|The centre is (0, 0)",
      "Your first script|`when green flag clicked` starts the program|`go to x: 0 y: 0` places the sprite|`say Hello for 2 seconds` shows a speech bubble"],
  a: "Explore Scratch: add a sprite and a backdrop, then write a script that starts at the centre, visits all four corners and says the coordinates at each one. Support: follow the six-step card. Stretch: make a second sprite visit the corners in the opposite order.",
  w: [["What is a sprite?", "A character or object that can be programmed."], ["What are the coordinates of the centre of the stage?", "(0, 0)."], ["Which block starts a program in Scratch?", "when green flag clicked."], ["A sprite is at x: 200, y: -150. Which corner is it near?", "Bottom right."], ["Explain why a program should set a sprite's starting position at the beginning.", "Otherwise it starts wherever it finished last time, so the program behaves differently on each run."]],
  x: ["What x value puts a sprite at the far left of the stage?", "-240."] },
{ t: "Sequence: animate a short story", o: "I can put blocks in the correct sequence to make an animation.",
  k: ["sequence: instructions carried out one after another in order", "script: a stack of blocks that runs from top to bottom", "costume: one of the pictures a sprite can show"],
  d: ["Put in order: say goodbye, walk to door, stand up.", "Stand up, walk to door, say goodbye."],
  s: ["Scripts run top to bottom|Each block finishes before the next starts|Changing the order changes the story|This is sequence",
      "Useful blocks|`say ... for 2 seconds` and `wait 1 seconds`|`glide 1 secs to x: y:` moves smoothly|`next costume` changes the picture",
      "Two sprites talking|Give each sprite its own script|Use `wait` blocks so they take turns|Plan the timing on paper first"],
  a: "Plan a three-scene story on a storyboard, then program it with two sprites that speak in turn and move. Support: build the conversation from the script on the card, then change the words. Stretch: add a third scene with a backdrop change.",
  w: [["What does sequence mean?", "Instructions carried out one after another in order."], ["Which block makes a sprite move smoothly to a position?", "glide ... secs to x: y:."], ["Two sprites speak at the same moment. Which block fixes this?", "A wait block before one of the speeches."], ["Predict: `say Hi for 2 seconds`, `move 50 steps`, `say Bye for 2 seconds`. Describe what you see.", "The sprite says Hi for 2 seconds, moves 50 steps, then says Bye for 2 seconds."], ["Explain what happens if the `move` block is put first, and why.", "The sprite moves before saying Hi, because blocks run from top to bottom."]],
  x: ["What is a script?", "A stack of blocks that runs from top to bottom."] },
{ t: "Lab: predict, run and modify", o: "I can predict what a script will do, test my prediction and change the script.",
  k: ["predict: to say what you think will happen before running", "modify: to change a program to do something different", "comment: a note in a program that explains it"],
  d: ["Why is it useful to predict what a program does before you run it?", "It makes you read the code carefully, and a wrong prediction shows you what you misunderstood."],
  s: ["Predict|Read every block of the starter animation|Write what you think will happen|Do not run it yet",
      "Run and investigate|Was your prediction right?|Click single blocks to see what each does|Add a comment to explain each script",
      "Modify|Change numbers first: speed, time, position|Then change the order|Then add something new"],
  a: "Open the starter animation. Write your prediction, run it, then complete the six modify challenges on the card (change speed, direction, colour, add a sound, add a sprite, change the ending). Support: complete the first three. Stretch: write a challenge of your own for a partner.",
  w: [["What does it mean to modify a program?", "To change it so it does something different."], ["What is a comment for?", "To explain what part of a program does, for people reading it."], ["`move 10 steps` is changed to `move -10 steps`. What happens?", "The sprite moves backwards."], ["You want a sprite to glide twice as fast. What do you change?", "Halve the number of seconds in the glide block."], ["Explain why changing one thing at a time is a good way to learn what a script does.", "You can see exactly what effect that one change has."]],
  x: ["Was your prediction correct? If not, what surprised you?", "An honest comparison."] },
{ t: "Events and keyboard control", o: "I can use events to make a sprite respond to the keyboard.",
  k: ["event: something that happens which a program can respond to", "event handler: the script that runs when an event happens", "direction: the way a sprite is facing, in degrees"],
  d: ["Name three things a player can do that a game must respond to.", "Press a key, click the mouse, touch the screen."],
  s: ["Events|A program waits for something to happen|Examples: key pressed, sprite clicked, flag clicked|Each event has its own script",
      "Moving with keys|`when right arrow key pressed`: `change x by 10`|`when left arrow key pressed`: `change x by -10`|Up and down change y",
      "Directions|0 is up, 90 is right, 180 is down, -90 is left|`point in direction 90` then `move 10 steps`|`set rotation style left-right` stops the sprite flipping upside down"],
  a: "Program a sprite to move in four directions with the arrow keys and say something when clicked. Add a second sprite controlled by W, A, S and D. Support: build right and left first using the card. Stretch: make the sprite face the direction it moves.",
  w: [["What is an event?", "Something that happens which a program can respond to."], ["Which block changes a sprite's left-right position?", "change x by."], ["Which direction number points a sprite to the right?", "90."], ["Write the two blocks that move a sprite down 10 when the down arrow is pressed.", "when down arrow key pressed; change y by -10."], ["Explain why each arrow key needs its own script.", "Each key is a separate event, so each needs its own event handler."]],
  x: ["Name two events used in your program.", "For example key pressed and sprite clicked."] },
{ t: "Iteration: forever and repeat", o: "I can use loops to repeat blocks.",
  k: ["iteration: repeating instructions", "forever loop: a loop that repeats until the program is stopped", "repeat loop: a loop that repeats a set number of times"],
  d: ["A script has `move 10 steps` written 20 times. How could it be shorter?", "Use repeat 20 around one move 10 steps block."],
  s: ["Why loop?|Fewer blocks to build and fix|`repeat 10` runs the blocks inside 10 times|`forever` keeps going until the red stop button",
      "Animation with loops|`forever`: `next costume`, `wait 0.2 seconds`|`repeat 36`: `turn 10 degrees` makes one full turn|36 x 10 = 360 degrees",
      "Bouncing|`forever`: `move 5 steps`, `if on edge, bounce`|The sprite keeps moving on its own|This is the engine of most games"],
  a: "Use repeat to draw a square and a triangle with the pen, then use forever to make a walking animation that bounces off the edges. Support: complete the square with the part-built script. Stretch: draw a spiral by changing the steps inside the loop.",
  w: [["What is iteration?", "Repeating instructions."], ["What is the difference between `repeat 10` and `forever`?", "repeat 10 runs ten times then stops. forever runs until the program is stopped."], ["How many degrees must a sprite turn at each corner to draw a square?", "90."], ["Complete: `repeat 3`: `move 100 steps`, `turn ___ degrees` draws a triangle.", "120."], ["A block is placed below a `forever` loop. Explain why it never runs.", "The forever loop never finishes, so the program never reaches the blocks after it."]],
  x: ["Which loop would you use for a game that runs until the player quits?", "forever."] },
{ t: "Lab: a sprite that moves and bounces", o: "I can combine events and loops in one program.",
  k: ["speed: how far a sprite moves each time through the loop", "bounce: to change direction on hitting an edge", "test: to run a program to check it works"],
  d: ["A sprite moves 5 steps each time round a forever loop. How could you make it move faster?", "Increase the number of steps."],
  s: ["Plan|A ball that moves by itself and bounces|A bat that the player moves left and right|Decide which sprite needs which scripts",
      "Build|Ball: `forever`, `move`, `if on edge, bounce`|Bat: left and right arrow events|Set starting positions when the flag is clicked",
      "Test|Does the ball ever get stuck?|Can the bat leave the screen?|Change one number at a time to tune the speed"],
  a: "Build the ball and bat. Make the ball start at a random direction and bounce around. Test it and record two problems you found and how you fixed them. Support: start from the project with the ball already moving. Stretch: stop the bat from leaving the stage.",
  w: [["Which block makes a sprite turn round at the edge of the stage?", "if on edge, bounce."], ["Which two block types have you combined today?", "Events and loops."], ["The ball moves too slowly. What do you change?", "Increase the steps in the move block."], ["Why set the ball's starting position under `when green flag clicked`?", "So every game starts the same way."], ["Explain how you would test whether the bat can leave the stage.", "Hold the arrow key until the bat reaches each edge and watch whether it disappears."]],
  x: ["Name one problem you found by testing.", "A specific problem."] },
{ t: "Selection: if and if-else, with sensing", o: "I can use if blocks so that a program makes decisions.",
  k: ["selection: choosing which blocks to run, based on a condition", "condition: a test that is true or false", "sensing: blocks that detect things, such as touching or a key being pressed"],
  d: ["'If it is break time, go outside, else stay in class.' What is the condition?", "It is break time."],
  s: ["If|`if <condition> then` runs the blocks inside only when the condition is true|Conditions are the pointed blocks|Example: `if touching Bat then`",
      "If-else|`if ... then ... else` gives two routes|One runs when true, the other when false|Example: if score > 9 say 'You win' else say 'Keep going'",
      "Check all the time|A condition is only tested when the program reaches it|Put the `if` inside a `forever` loop so it keeps checking|This is the most common Scratch bug"],
  a: "Add selection to your ball and bat: if the ball touches the bat it bounces upwards; if it touches the red line at the bottom, say 'Missed'. Support: follow the card to add the first if block. Stretch: use if-else to change the ball's colour depending on which half of the stage it is in.",
  w: [["What is selection?", "Choosing which blocks to run, based on a condition."], ["What shape are condition blocks in Scratch?", "Pointed (hexagonal)."], ["Name one sensing block.", "For example touching, touching color, key pressed."], ["When does the else part of an if-else run?", "When the condition is false."], ["An `if touching Bat` block is placed straight after the green flag block and never seems to work. Explain why and give the fix.", "It is tested only once, at the start. Put it inside a forever loop so it is checked repeatedly."]],
  x: ["Write one condition from your game.", "For example touching Bat."] },
{ t: "Collisions and game rules", o: "I can program what happens when sprites touch.",
  k: ["collision: when two sprites touch", "rule: what the game does in a certain situation", "random: chosen by chance"],
  d: ["In a catching game, what should happen when the falling object touches the basket?", "The object disappears or resets to the top, and the score goes up."],
  s: ["Detecting a collision|`if touching Basket then`|Inside a forever loop|Decide what happens to each sprite",
      "A falling object|`go to x: (pick random -220 to 220) y: 170` starts at the top|`forever`: `change y by -5`|If y is below -170, go back to the top",
      "Rules to write down|Caught: reset to the top, play a sound|Missed: reset to the top|Later: change score and lives"],
  a: "Start the catching game: an apple falls from a random position; a basket moves with the arrow keys; when caught or missed the apple returns to the top. Support: begin with the falling apple script on the card. Stretch: add a second falling object that should be avoided.",
  w: [["What is a collision?", "When two sprites touch."], ["Which block picks a number by chance?", "pick random."], ["Why should the apple start at a random x position?", "So the game is different each time and the player cannot predict it."], ["Write the condition that is true when the apple has fallen past the bottom.", "y position < -170."], ["Explain why collision checks belong inside the forever loop that moves the apple.", "The apple's position changes every time round the loop, so the check must happen each time."]],
  x: ["Write one rule of your game as an 'if ... then ...' sentence.", "For example: if the apple touches the basket then it returns to the top."] },
{ t: "Lab: catch-the-object game, version 1", o: "I can build and test a working game with movement and collisions.",
  k: ["version: one stage in the development of a program", "playtest: to play a game in order to find problems", "bug report: a note describing a problem and how to make it happen"],
  d: ["What three things must version 1 of the catching game do?", "Object falls, player moves, collision is detected."],
  s: ["Finish version 1|Falling object with a random start|Player control with the arrow keys|Caught and missed both reset the object",
      "Playtest|Play for two minutes|Try to break it: hold keys, go to the edges|Note anything unexpected",
      "Write a bug report|What happened?|What did you expect?|How can someone make it happen again?"],
  a: "Complete version 1 and save it. Swap computers with a partner, playtest their game for two minutes and write one bug report. Fix the bug reported in your own game. Support: use the version 1 checklist. Stretch: add a start screen that waits for the space key.",
  w: [["What is a playtest?", "Playing a game in order to find problems."], ["What three things should a bug report say?", "What happened, what was expected, how to reproduce it."], ["The object sometimes falls through the basket. Suggest one cause.", "It moves too many steps at a time, or the check is not inside the loop."], ["Why save your work as 'version 1' before adding more?", "So there is a working copy to go back to if the next changes break it."], ["Explain why another person often finds bugs that the programmer missed.", "They play in unexpected ways and do not know what the programmer intended."]],
  x: ["Write the bug that was reported in your game.", "A specific bug."] },
{ t: "Variables: score, lives and timer", o: "I can use variables to keep score, count lives and time a game.",
  k: ["variable: a named store for a value that can change", "initialise: to give a variable its starting value", "increment: to increase a variable, usually by one"],
  d: ["A game shows 'Score: 0'. Where does the game keep that number while you play?", "In a variable."],
  s: ["Variables in Scratch|Make a variable and give it a clear name: Score|`set Score to 0` initialises it|`change Score by 1` increments it",
      "Using variables|Set Score to 0 and Lives to 3 when the flag is clicked|Caught: change Score by 1|Missed: change Lives by -1",
      "A timer|`set Time to 30`|`repeat until Time = 0`: `wait 1 seconds`, `change Time by -1`|Then end the game"],
  a: "Add Score and Lives to your catching game, initialised when the flag is clicked. Add a 30-second countdown timer. Support: add Score first using the card. Stretch: make the object fall faster as the score increases.",
  w: [["What is a variable?", "A named store for a value that can change."], ["Which block gives a variable its starting value?", "set ... to."], ["Lives is 3. The block `change Lives by -1` runs twice. What is Lives?", "1."], ["Why must Score be set to 0 when the flag is clicked?", "Otherwise it keeps the value from the last game."], ["Explain the difference between `set Score to 1` and `change Score by 1`.", "set makes Score exactly 1 whatever it was. change adds 1 to its current value."]],
  x: ["Name the variables in your game and their starting values.", "For example Score 0, Lives 3, Time 30."] },
{ t: "Broadcasts and levels", o: "I can use broadcasts so that sprites work together.",
  k: ["broadcast: a message sent to all sprites", "receive: to respond to a broadcast", "level: a stage of a game, usually harder than the one before"],
  d: ["When a game ends, several things happen at once: the music stops, a message appears, sprites hide. How might one event tell every sprite?", "Send one message that all of them listen for."],
  s: ["Broadcasts|`broadcast game over` sends a message|Any sprite can have `when I receive game over`|One event triggers many scripts",
      "Game over|`if Lives < 1 then broadcast game over`|Game Over sprite: show when it receives the message|Other sprites: hide and stop",
      "Levels|`if Score = 10 then broadcast level 2`|Backdrop switches; the object falls faster|Use a variable called Speed so it is easy to change"],
  a: "Add a game over broadcast when Lives reaches 0, and a level 2 broadcast at Score 10 that changes the backdrop and speed. Support: add game over only. Stretch: add a level 3 and a win screen.",
  w: [["What is a broadcast?", "A message sent to all sprites."], ["Which block makes a sprite respond to a broadcast?", "when I receive."], ["Write the condition that should trigger game over.", "Lives < 1 (or Lives = 0)."], ["Why use a variable called Speed instead of typing the number into the move block?", "It can be changed in one place, for example when the level changes."], ["Explain why broadcasts are better than copying the same 'end game' blocks into every sprite.", "The decision is made once, and every sprite reacts to it. Changes are made in one place."]],
  x: ["Name one broadcast in your game and which sprites receive it.", "For example game over, received by the apple and the Game Over sprite."] },
{ t: "Lab: scoring and a game-over screen", o: "I can finish and test a complete game with scoring and an ending.",
  k: ["complete: having everything that is needed", "test plan: a list of tests and their expected results", "expected result: what should happen if the program is correct"],
  d: ["Name three tests you would run on a game with Score and Lives.", "Catch one: score +1. Miss one: lives -1. Miss three: game over."],
  s: ["Finish|Score and Lives shown on the stage|Game over screen appears and play stops|Everything resets when the flag is clicked again",
      "Test plan|Write the test: 'miss three apples'|Write the expected result: 'game over appears'|Run it and tick or cross",
      "Fix|If a test fails, find the script responsible|Change one thing|Run the same test again"],
  a: "Complete the catching game and fill in the six-row test plan with expected and actual results. Fix any failed test. Support: use the test plan with the tests already written. Stretch: add a high score that is remembered between games.",
  w: [["What is a test plan?", "A list of tests with their expected results."], ["Write the expected result for the test 'catch one apple'.", "Score increases by 1 and the apple returns to the top."], ["After game over, the apple keeps falling. Which block is missing?", "A stop or hide when the apple receives game over."], ["You click the flag and the score starts at 7. What is missing?", "set Score to 0 at the start."], ["Explain why you should test what happens at exactly 0 lives.", "It is the boundary where the game must end. Mistakes such as using < 0 only show there."]],
  x: ["How many of your six tests passed first time?", "A number out of 6."] },
{ t: "Design your own game", o: "I can plan a game with clear rules and success criteria.",
  k: ["design: a plan of what will be made", "success criteria: what the finished game must do", "user: the person who will play the game"],
  d: ["Think of a simple game you enjoy. What is the player's goal?", "A clear goal, such as reach the end, collect items, or survive as long as possible."],
  s: ["A good plan|Goal: what must the player do?|Sprites: what are they and what does each do?|Rules: how do you score, lose and win?",
      "Keep it achievable|Three or four sprites at most|It must use a loop, a decision, a variable and an event|You have two lessons to build it",
      "Success criteria|Write five 'The game must...' statements|These become your tests|Example: 'The game must end when lives reach 0'"],
  a: "Complete the game design sheet: goal, sketch of the stage, sprite table, rules and five success criteria. Get it checked by a partner before building. Support: choose one of the three game templates (maze, catch, dodge). Stretch: plan a second level with a new rule.",
  w: [["What is the goal of your game?", "A clear one-sentence goal."], ["List your sprites and what each one does.", "A list of sprites with roles."], ["Which variable will your game use and what is it for?", "For example Score to count points."], ["Write one rule that needs an `if` block.", "For example: if the player touches the wall then go back to the start."], ["Write two success criteria in the form 'The game must...'.", "Two testable statements."]],
  x: ["What is the first thing you will build next lesson?", "A specific first step, such as player movement."] },
{ t: "Build sprint 1", o: "I can build the core of my game by following my plan.",
  k: ["sprint: a short, focused period of building", "core: the most important part that everything else depends on", "save point: a saved working copy"],
  d: ["What is the 'core' of a maze game?", "The player moving and being stopped by walls."],
  s: ["Core first|Player movement|The main rule: one collision that matters|Leave decoration until later",
      "Work in small steps|Add one script|Test it|Save before the next step",
      "When stuck|Read the script aloud block by block|Check whether the `if` is inside a loop|Try three fixes before asking"],
  a: "Build the core of your game: the player sprite, its controls and the main collision rule. Save as version 1. Support: build from your chosen template. Stretch: also add the scoring variable.",
  w: [["What did you build first, and why?", "The core, such as movement, because everything depends on it."], ["Write one script from your game in words.", "A clear description of an event, loop or decision."], ["What did you test after adding it?", "A specific test."], ["Describe one problem you met and how you solved it.", "A specific problem and solution."], ["What will you build in sprint 2?", "The next features from the plan."]],
  x: ["Which of your success criteria does your game already meet?", "A list from the student's criteria."] },
{ t: "Lab: build sprint 2 with peer testing", o: "I can complete my game and improve it using feedback from a tester.",
  k: ["feature: one thing a program can do", "peer testing: someone else playing your game to find problems", "priority: what is most important to do first"],
  d: ["You have 20 minutes and three things left to add. How do you choose what to do first?", "Do the one the game most needs to meet its success criteria."],
  s: ["Sprint 2|Add scoring, losing and winning|Check each success criterion|Save as version 2",
      "Peer testing|Swap games|Play for three minutes without help|Fill in the testing form: works, breaks, suggestion",
      "Use the feedback|Fix things that break first|Then confusing things|Then nice extras"],
  a: "Complete the remaining features, then swap with a partner for peer testing using the form. Fix the highest-priority problem they found. Support: aim to meet three of your five criteria. Stretch: add sound and a difficulty increase.",
  w: [["Which features did you add today?", "A list of features."], ["What did your tester say worked well?", "A specific comment."], ["What problem did your tester find?", "A specific problem."], ["What did you change because of the feedback?", "A specific change."], ["Explain how you decided which problem to fix first.", "By priority: problems that break the game before cosmetic ones."]],
  x: ["How many of your success criteria are met now?", "A number out of 5."] },
{ t: "Fix bugs and add comments", o: "I can fix the remaining bugs and explain my code with comments.",
  k: ["comment: a note that explains code to a reader", "readable: easy for another person to understand", "tidy: to arrange scripts neatly"],
  d: ["Why might you not understand your own program in three months?", "You forget what each part was for unless it is explained."],
  s: ["Fix|List the remaining bugs from peer testing|Fix the most serious first|Retest after each fix",
      "Comment|Right-click a script and add a comment|Say what the script is for, not what each block is|Example: 'Ends the game when lives run out'",
      "Tidy|Give sprites and variables sensible names|Delete unused scripts|Line scripts up so they do not overlap"],
  a: "Fix your remaining bugs, add a comment to every script, and rename any sprites still called Sprite1. Support: comment your three most important scripts. Stretch: write a short 'how to play' screen.",
  w: [["What is a comment?", "A note that explains code to a reader."], ["Which is the better comment for a script: 'forever loop' or 'Moves the ball and bounces it off the walls'? Why?", "The second: it says what the script is for."], ["Why rename Sprite1 to something like Player?", "So the program is easier to read and broadcasts and conditions make sense."], ["Write a comment for the script that changes your score.", "For example: Adds one to the score when the apple is caught."], ["Explain how comments help the person who will assess your game.", "They show that you understand what each part does."]],
  x: ["Which bug was hardest to fix, and what was the cause?", "A specific bug and cause."] },
{ t: "Assessed showcase and code explanation", o: "I can demonstrate my game and explain how its code works.",
  k: ["demonstrate: to show something working", "explain: to say how or why something works", "terminology: the correct technical words"],
  d: ["Find one loop and one decision in your game. Be ready to explain them.", "The student's own examples."],
  s: ["The showcase|Play your game for the class or your table|Say the goal and the controls|Two minutes each",
      "The code explanation|Point to one selection and one loop|Say what each does and why it is needed|Use the words condition, variable, event",
      "How you are assessed|The game meets its success criteria|The code uses sequence, selection, iteration and a variable|Your explanation is accurate"],
  a: "Present your game, then complete the written code explanation: describe one selection and one loop from your own code using the correct terms. Support: use the sentence starters. Stretch: explain one thing you would redesign and why.",
  w: [["State the goal of your game.", "A clear goal."], ["Describe one loop in your game and what it repeats.", "An accurate description using the word loop or iteration."], ["Describe one decision in your game. What is the condition?", "An accurate description with a condition."], ["Name a variable and say when it changes.", "A variable and the event that changes it."], ["Explain what would go wrong if your main `if` block were moved outside its loop.", "It would be checked only once, so the game would not respond after the start."]],
  x: ["Which success criterion are you proudest of meeting?", "One criterion."] },
{ t: "Feedback and reflection", o: "I can use feedback to improve my game and reflect on what I learned.",
  k: ["feedback: comments that help you improve", "strength: something done well", "next step: the most useful thing to improve"],
  d: ["Name the three building blocks of programs that your game uses.", "Sequence, selection, iteration."],
  s: ["Read your feedback|Find your strength and your next step|Check your level for each outcome|Ask about anything unclear",
      "Improve|Make the change your teacher suggested|Test it|Save as the final version",
      "Looking ahead|Later this year you will write the same ideas in Python|Blocks become typed words: `if`, `while`, `for`|The thinking stays the same"],
  a: "Improve your game using the feedback and complete the unit reflection. Support: your teacher has named the one script to change. Stretch: write the main loop of your game as a flowchart.",
  w: [["Copy your next step and say what you changed.", "The feedback and the change."], ["Name one thing you can do now that you could not do six weeks ago.", "A unit skill."], ["Describe one bug and how you found it.", "A specific bug and method."], ["Which was hardest: sequence, selection, iteration or variables? Why?", "A reasoned choice."], ["Set one target for your next programming unit.", "A specific target."]],
  x: ["In one sentence, how do sequence, selection and iteration make a game work?", "Steps run in order, decisions respond to what happens, and loops keep the game running."] }
] }); })(typeof module !== "undefined" ? require("../lessons.js") : window.Toolkit);
